 First, we apply the AM-GM inequality to the denominators $(x + y)^2$ and $(y + z)^2.$ This gives us
\begin{align*}
(x + y)^2 &\geq 4xy \\
(y + z)^2 &\geq 4yz.
\end{align*}
Multiplying these inequalities together, we obtain
\begin{align*}
(x + y)^2 (y + z)^2 &\geq 16xyz.
\end{align*}
Dividing both sides by 16, we get
\begin{align*}
\frac{(x + y)^2 (y + z)^2}{16} &\geq xyz.
\end{align*}
Therefore, the expression we are trying to maximize is at most
\frac{xyz(x + y + z)}{16} = \frac{x + y + z}{16}.
Since $x, y,$ and $z$ are positive real numbers, the minimum value that the numerator $x + y + z$ can take is 3, which occurs when $x = y = z = 1.$ Therefore, the maximum value of the expression is $\boxed{\frac{3}{16}}.$
